AFS+ for AROS
=============

This is the AFS+ filesystem handler for AROS, built for @CPU@ by the build
profile @PROFILE_ID@, with filesystem programs that manage an AFS+ volume from
the machine itself. It is one handler for every AROS machine: the same source
serves Hosted MacAROS on AArch64 and an AROS PC on x86_64, and each profile
says below what has been run on it.

AFS+ is a new filesystem for AROS: 64-bit throughout, journalled, with
extents, clones of a file that share their blocks until one of them is
written, extended attributes and a change stream programs can watch. It reads
and writes its own format only; it is not a Fast File System replacement that
understands old volumes.

This release is alpha.
@QUALIFICATION@


What is in here
---------------

  L/afsplus-handler            the filesystem itself, an AROS module
  C/AFSPlusSnapshot            create/list/delete and view local captures
  C/AFSPlusBackup              consistent local volume snapshot capture
  C/AFSPlusRestore             restore a file or directory into a fresh name
  C/AFSPlusReclaim             bounded private restore-stage maintenance
  C/AFSPlusInfo                what a mounted AFS+ volume is doing
  C/AFSPlusTour                a tour of clones, watches and attributes
  C/AFSPlusClone               clone a file, or copy it if the volume cannot
  C/AFSPlusDriverProbe         what a block device promises the handler
  Devs/DOSDrivers/AFSPLUS.example  a mountlist to edit and rename
  ReadMe                       this file
  SHA256SUMS                   the hash of every file above
  abi-report.txt               the machine-code audit of the handler
  build-profile.txt            exactly which toolchain and inputs built it


Installing it
-------------

  Copy L/afsplus-handler to L:
  Copy C/#? to C:
  Copy Devs/DOSDrivers/AFSPLUS.example to DEVS:DOSDrivers/AFSPLUS

Then edit DEVS:DOSDrivers/AFSPLUS: it is commented, and the lines you have to
change are Device, Unit and the geometry of the partition. Mount it with

  Mount AFSPLUS:

A volume is made on a host with mkafsplus or through explicit DOS Format.
Formatting replaces filesystem state. Persistent snapshots are selected by
mkafsplus --snapshots, or by FORMATSNAPSHOTS=YES in the DOSDriver Control
string for explicit Format. The feature is fixed when the volume is formatted.
Supply SNAPSHOTEDIT, SNAPSHOTVIEWS and SNAPSHOTRECLAIM positive runtime budgets
together to mount that volume; examples are in the DOSDriver template.
BACKUP and RESTORE each default to DENY. ROOT uses current security.library
ownership; SINGLEUSER declares an administrator domain without that library.

AFSPlusBackup DEVICE captures a registered local snapshot. LIST enumerates
captures. AFSPlusSnapshot DEVICE VIEW ID [PATH] reads an immutable view.
AFSPlusReclaim DEVICE DIRECTORY [CURSORFILE [SCAN WORK DEPTH]] performs one
authorized restore-maintenance step. It finds private retained stages by their
managed markers, never by their names. Repeat while complete=0; defaults scan
64 entries, perform 8 object/extent steps, and traverse 64 levels. Cursor files
must be outside the repaired volume; the default is RAM:AFSPlusReclaim.cursor.
The command checks and pins the resolved cursor parent directory, so an assign
or symlink cannot redirect cursor writes into the repaired volume.
On a stale cursor remove that cursor and restart on a quiescent subtree. An
interruption retains durable progress; losing the cursor permits another scan.
blocked>0 reports partial completion and keeps malformed stages private. Select
a deeper live DIRECTORY when the traversal depth bound is reached. Cursor save
flushes and closes a private file, then deletes the old cursor and renames the
complete replacement. A crash in that gap loses only the cursor; rescan safely.
This command does not inspect or expose
historical payloads, and it never removes published objects or snapshot data.

AFSPlusRestore DEVICE ID PATH DIRECTORY NEWNAME restores a file or directory
into a new destination, including supported metadata. Directory operations
admit at most 4096 nodes, 64 levels and 4 MiB of aggregate names/comments/
attributes; optional NODES DEPTH METADATABYTES arguments set explicit budgets.
Hard-linked files, symlinks and security descriptors are refused before target
mutation. Each complete directory is published through one final rename. External
archives and system boot-root rollback have separate interfaces.


Two things worth knowing
------------------------

COMMIT= says how long a change may wait before it reaches the disk. The
default, COMMIT=5, lets five seconds of changes gather, which is what makes
writing many small files fast. A power cut loses at most the changes still
waiting, whole and in order: the volume is never half written. A partition
you boot from should use COMMIT=SYNC, which lets nothing wait.

CACHE=AUTO, the default, sizes the cache from the machine's free memory and
treats Buffers as a floor. CACHE=BUFFERS takes Buffers exactly, for a machine
where memory is the scarce thing.

Both go in the Control line of the DOSDriver. The file lists the rest.


